WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.377
To understand what happened in the midterm
elections,

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:04.254
take a look at this chart.

00:00:04.254 --> 00:00:06.756
Two years ago, Trump made his biggest gains

00:00:06.756 --> 00:00:09.092
where wages had fallen the most.

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:13.471
In this election, low-income counties
shifted Democratic by over 20 points.

00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:17.225
Republicans’ advantage in rural America
shrank by double digits.

00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:18.893
So what happened?

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:22.355
The media story is that Trump lost working people.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:23.023
That's true.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:26.109
But if you follow More Perfect Union,
that's pretty obvious.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:30.113
On the surface, Democrats seem to be
the party of the working class again.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:34.576
The real fight in this country is not left
versus right,

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:36.995
it is top versus bottom.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:38.872
We showed that in a battle between 

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:39.998
the money and the many

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:41.958
that the many really can win.

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:44.085
In a politics dominated by billionaires 

00:00:44.085 --> 00:00:45.545
and special interest groups,

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:48.173
we will not bow down.

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:51.426
[Faiz Shakir]
But this movement could be either short-lived or durable.

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:56.347
From what I've seen on the ground,
in exit polls and in campaign spending,

00:00:56.347 --> 00:01:00.268
we'll discuss what could make this
a political realignment

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:01.686
rather than a reaction,

00:01:01.686 --> 00:01:06.274
and what Democrats could do right now to
deliver on their biggest campaign promise:

00:01:06.274 --> 00:01:10.320
Affordability.

00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:15.825
[Faiz Shakir]
Here’s one more chart from data analyst G. Elliott Morris.

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:19.412
It maps month by month
how often members of Congress

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:22.415
were using
the defining word of this election.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.334
And it raises the question:

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:26.086
What happened in mid-2025?

00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:29.798
At the time, during Trump's
first year of his second term,

00:01:30.131 --> 00:01:33.676
two different visions were playing out
within the Democratic Party.

00:01:34.135 --> 00:01:37.806
As Donald Trump and Elon Musk
were torching government services...

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:38.348
[Elon Musk]
Chainsaw!

00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:41.101
[Faiz Shakir]
...firing government employees...

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:44.229
[President Donald Trump]
That’s not a job that’s going to make America great again.

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:46.606
[Faiz Shakir]
...and courting the nation’s elite,

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:50.777
one faction of the Democratic Party
said, play dead, do nothing.

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:54.405
[James Carville]
My point is, just lay back.

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:55.240
Play possum.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:58.451
And arose a different faction
within the Democratic Party,

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:02.747
the economic populists who said, 
“Fight the oligarchy.”

00:02:03.164 --> 00:02:07.085
[Sen. Bernie Sanders]
You know, I used to talk about oligarchy
and people’d say, “What is he talking about?”

00:02:07.252 --> 00:02:10.255
Everybody knows what I'm talking
about tonight.

00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:12.006
[Faiz Shakir]
At the same time,

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:15.385
 Zohran Mamdani was polling at all of 1% in New York.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.221
But of course, that would soon change.

00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:22.684
[Zohran Mamdani]
Tonight you have delivered a mandate for change,

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:26.312
a mandate for a new kind of politics,

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:30.441
a mandate for a city we can afford,

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:34.445
and a mandate for a government
that delivers exactly that.

00:02:35.947 --> 00:02:39.409
[Faiz Shakir]
What Zohran Mamdani’s amazing campaign 
in New York showed

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:42.704
is how to win working-class people across the spectrum,

00:02:43.037 --> 00:02:45.957
even as he was derided by big-moneyed interests

00:02:45.957 --> 00:02:49.210
and many of the media
elite as being a far-left agenda.

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:50.837
[Fox News host]
And I'm sure Republicans

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:54.090
look at what Zohran Mamdani
just said yesterday and say, please say

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.635
more of that, because that does give us
an opening in the midterm elections.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.306
[Faiz Shakir]
Those who were paying attention
keenly were taking notes,

00:03:01.723 --> 00:03:04.726
and they were changing
the way that they were talking.

00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:07.562
James Carville, by late November, is saying

00:03:07.562 --> 00:03:11.149
that economic populism
needs to be the message of all Democrats.

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.943
And the campaigns started to change, too.

00:03:13.943 --> 00:03:16.613
[James Talarico]
The only minority destroying

00:03:16.613 --> 00:03:18.823
America is the billionaires.

00:03:18.823 --> 00:03:23.578
[Faiz Shakir]
This chart of ad spending on issue
by issue is a good shorthand for how much

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:26.956
Democrats centered affordability
and economic populism this year.

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:31.628
Two years ago, the top ad issues
for Democrats looked very different,

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:38.551
with Trump's character among the top five
issues for both Senate and House race ad buys.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:42.430
That economic prism of talking about class-based

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.183
issues pervaded all Democratic campaigns.

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:47.477
Gallons of gas, gallons of milk.

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:50.063
Trump and Rogers could care less
how much these things cost.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:53.775
I am so tired of working people being told
that we should settle for scraps

00:03:53.775 --> 00:03:56.402
while the richest people in this country
get permanent relief.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:57.779
That is all backwards.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:01.032
Across Alaska, we are getting crushed by corporate greed.

00:04:01.032 --> 00:04:03.910
The biggest corporations and the richest Americans

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:07.372
 have grabbed unimaginable wealth at your expense.

00:04:07.622 --> 00:04:08.623
In my view,

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:11.501
economic populism is defined by a few core things.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:16.089
First, you have to be willing
to take on people with power.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.925
To drive private equity out of
housing, health care, nursing homes.

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.051
Name the villain.

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:24.305
We are all being taken advantage
of by Big Tech, by these data centers.

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:25.723
I'm Dr. Annie Andrews.

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:29.852
I'll go after big insurance companies
overcharging you and ripping us off.

00:04:29.852 --> 00:04:31.604
When Big Pharma raised your bills,

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:32.689
I took them on.

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:33.606
Who are we up against?

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:35.775
Who is depriving us of economic power?

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.778
Elon Musk wants our land.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.446
He wants our water.

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:42.615
He wants our tax dollars.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:46.160
And he wants his very own
personal United States senator.

00:04:46.577 --> 00:04:50.623
Second: what specific agenda items
do you want to put on the docket

00:04:50.915 --> 00:04:53.876
to improve the lives of  working-class Americans?

00:04:53.876 --> 00:04:55.920
— Money out of?
— Politics!

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.422
— Money in your?
— Pocket!

00:04:57.422 --> 00:04:58.756
— Medicare for?
— All!

00:04:58.756 --> 00:05:00.049
That's right.

00:05:00.049 --> 00:05:02.552
Ban them from denying care a doctor says you need.

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:05.638
I want to expand Medicare to cover
vision, dental and hearing.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:07.807
Stopping
these stupid wars around the world

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:12.103
so that we can bring our money
and resources home and invest in housing

00:05:12.103 --> 00:05:15.565
we can all afford, health care we can
count on and Social Security.

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:20.945
And third: how are you building a grassroots movement
that reflects the emotions

00:05:20.945 --> 00:05:24.949
of the working-class
people, their anger, their frustrations?

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:29.162
In this election cycle, Republicans
largely hid from public view.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.456
When there were town halls, he walked out of that.

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:35.752
So you're not actually listening
and hearing people's pain.

00:05:35.752 --> 00:05:37.462
[Faiz]
Abdul El-Sayed held over 

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:40.423
700 public events in Michigan alone.

00:05:40.423 --> 00:05:43.343
[Adam Hamilton]
If any of you were angry and upset,
I was going to listen to that, too.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:45.762
And I've had church people angry
and upset with me.

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:47.805
I don't walk out of the room
when people are angry or upset.

00:05:47.805 --> 00:05:50.892
James Talarico did hundreds of events in Texas.

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:55.855
So, you stay here, 
you answer our questions, or you suffer the consequences.

00:05:55.855 --> 00:06:00.526
I appreciate you being here and I appreciate you traveling in and I appreciate...

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:01.986
I did it for my people, not for you.

00:06:01.986 --> 00:06:03.613
And I think that’s wonderful.

00:06:03.613 --> 00:06:05.239
It’s all right. It’s all right. It’s all right.

00:06:05.239 --> 00:06:08.534
This is—
this is what this conversation is supposed to be about.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:12.121
This is how Democrats won,
by talking to working-class people,

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:15.333
hearing their pain, anger and frustration,

00:06:15.416 --> 00:06:19.921
and channeling that towards
an economic agenda to improve their lives.

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:24.050
One cautionary note about the Election
Day outcomes

00:06:24.092 --> 00:06:26.969
is that while individual
Democratic candidates did well,

00:06:26.969 --> 00:06:31.349
the Democratic Party brand is still suffering, 
with majorities disapproving of it.

00:06:31.349 --> 00:06:35.019
Democratic Party leadership did not come forward

00:06:35.186 --> 00:06:39.107
with a specific agenda to offer working-class 
people in this election cycle.

00:06:39.273 --> 00:06:43.569
It was left to individual
candidates themselves to form agendas,

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:46.197
to speak to the working-class
people of their states.

00:06:46.197 --> 00:06:49.158
The opportunity
now is to present an agenda

00:06:49.158 --> 00:06:51.953
that speaks for a Democratic Party
writ large.

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:54.956
And it's not going to come from
the Democratic leadership that we have.

00:06:55.832 --> 00:06:58.292
Here are five things Democrats should do

00:06:58.292 --> 00:07:01.295
if they want to remain the party
of the working class.

00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:04.465
These agenda items,
mind you, are wildly popular

00:07:04.465 --> 00:07:07.885
in Texas, Georgia, Kansas,
Michigan and everywhere else

00:07:07.885 --> 00:07:10.179
the Democrats won on election night.

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:12.014
First, end the wars.

00:07:12.306 --> 00:07:14.225
If we're going to be serious
about ending the wars,

00:07:14.225 --> 00:07:16.978
we need to leverage ending the funding
for those wars.

00:07:16.978 --> 00:07:21.524
Second, raise the cap on taxable
income on Social Security.

00:07:21.524 --> 00:07:24.235
Make billionaires pay their fair share
so that we can expand

00:07:24.235 --> 00:07:27.113
Social Security benefits for working-class
Americans.

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:30.908
Third, raise
the minimum wage to a living wage.

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:34.120
Fourth, break up big medicine's

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:37.331
corporate monopolies and provide
better health care for more people.

00:07:37.665 --> 00:07:38.749
And fifth,

00:07:38.749 --> 00:07:41.627
rein in the power of the Big Tech
oligarchs,

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:44.547
protect communities from unchecked data
center development

00:07:44.547 --> 00:07:46.466
 and create new AI safety guardrails.

00:07:49.343 --> 00:07:53.139
We sent a crystal-clear message to the people at the top.

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.308
We will not be conquered. 

00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:59.061
Michigan, we did it!

00:07:59.061 --> 00:08:01.689
I think we just made some history.

00:08:01.689 --> 00:08:02.940
[Faiz]
Let's be clear.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:06.402
The biggest overriding
issue of this election cycle was Donald

00:08:06.402 --> 00:08:09.989
Trump's corruption and his lack of action
on anything to improve

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:11.991
working-class people's lives.

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:15.578
In order for economic populists to win against this backdrop,

00:08:15.578 --> 00:08:19.665
you had to provide a sense
that you were focused on economic issues.

00:08:19.916 --> 00:08:23.961
And against a media discourse that is often
 pitting identity politics

00:08:23.961 --> 00:08:26.923
to distract one candidate or another,

00:08:27.048 --> 00:08:31.052
we saw across the board, candidates
focused on talking about health care,

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:35.765
talking about the economy, talking about
AI and data center development.

00:08:36.057 --> 00:08:39.227
They were doing
so in ways that were focused and attentive

00:08:39.227 --> 00:08:41.521
to economic pain and suffering.

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:44.649
They may have talked about those issues
in slightly different ways.

00:08:44.649 --> 00:08:47.151
Not all candidates
were for Medicare for all,

00:08:47.151 --> 00:08:50.112
but all candidates were talking
about health care expansion.

00:08:50.112 --> 00:08:54.242
Not all candidates were for taxing the rich
in specific ways, but all candidates

00:08:54.242 --> 00:08:58.162
were for raising the tax rates
on the wealthiest in society.

00:08:58.162 --> 00:09:03.501
Discussing economic justice issues
and being focused on economic populism

00:09:03.501 --> 00:09:07.004
while Donald Trump had nothing to offer
the working-class people of this country

00:09:07.171 --> 00:09:10.675
provided the path
to win enormous victories.

